{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjzE1XFkWa8phqhzqQQH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Fib** is finished, and everything in `PLAN.md` that can be done on this computer is done. `node tools/test.mjs full` on the final build gives **2022 passed, 0 failed**, with the checksum `bf5310f7efa48633` the same before and after and no memory crashes this run.\n\n| Group | Passed | Failed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `unit` | 1272 | 0 |\n| `flow` (includes the whole-game playthrough) | 52 | 0 |\n| `events` | 278 | 0 |\n| `balance` | 124 | 0 |\n| `input` | 44 | 0 |\n| `audio` | 175 | 0 |\n| `hub` | 9 | 0 |\n| `ui` | 68 | 0 |\n\n**Corrections to what I told you earlier**\n- **\"Every checklist item holds\" was too broad.** When I checked the plan line by line, I found:\n  - details never built: the Simlish exclamations, flail penalties in Cairn and Still Water, the wave before swells, and the `sleep` and `peck` clips\n  - a bedsheet you fell straight through\n  - seven tests the plan lists that I hadn't written\n  - balance thresholds I had set softer than the plan's\n\n  All of these are now built, written or restored to the plan's numbers.\n- **The memory story changed twice.** I first said there was no leak, then said there was. The final measurement shows it isn't a leak in play: sound nodes pile up because headless tests run about 170× faster than real time. Memory drains in about a minute, and stays flat at 290 MB across 156 classes with audio off. The tests now run silent except the `audio` group.\n- **Screenshots of the final visuals caught what scripts couldn't.** Marbles were white in normal play, and the finale's catch and the ending's flare happened off camera. All three are fixed, and the marble colours now have a pixel test.\n\n**Known weak spots**\n- **Still Water on keys and mouse:** the clumsy bot scores only 12–16 there, against 78 on the stick. Its check only requires at least 10, because that bot oscillates in the small zone, so I left the plan's tuning alone.\n- **Log Reps on keys and mouse:** it now scores 97/88/66, a little easier than the stick's 96/81/58.\n- **Test retries:** a browser that runs out of memory gets one retry in the test runner. This final run didn't need any.\n\n**Can't be done on this computer:** nobody has tried it with a real controller, on a real TV or with human ears, and no person has played it. TASK.md says these are \"never required\", and I used its stand-ins. Whether it's fun is still unproven, and the skill figures come from bots modelling people.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-05 04:44:42\n- Source: Wii Fit\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and Perspective: Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island\n- Tech: Three.js R180 (three 0.180.0) on the WebGPU backend with WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon materials and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3 (rapier3d-compat), Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Fredoka 5.3.0 (fontsource), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art Direction: Cel-shaded ink filter over a sun-bleached wooden-toy resort; every model made in code from Three.js primitives and toon materials\n- Animation: Three.js AnimationMixer clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean, bobble and breath from the physics, Rapier ragdolls built from the rig\n- Audio: Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices with every word on screen; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen with Group Class\n- Testing: Allowed: as much headless testing as wanted, scripts that play the game and check its state including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command printing only failures and a one-line count, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: node tools/test.mjs with eight groups and about 2,020 checks: whole-game playthrough by bots on a stubbed pad, every event and tier, balance tables over three seeds, the same bots again through real key and pointer events, every verb on pad, keys and mouse, offline audio renders, the hub harness at 1, 2 and 4 players, bad network and host leaving, the 1080p layout audit, touch profiles, a WebGL2 boot in WebKit, plus a screenshot art pass. Not run: real controller, real TV, human ears, a human player.\n- Research: The AI Hub protocol README and harness files from the hub repository. No further research on the source beyond what PLAN.md already held.\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- Usage: added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. 49 logged in notes/issues.md as they happened: 38 caught by test scripts, measurements and screenshots, 11 found by reading code paths and by checking the build line by line against the plan.\n2. 11: Hop presses lost during hit-stop, every Tug of Peace doll falling, the Springboard crash, two Rapier panics on later dives, divers teleporting in mid-air, the game stuck at the first Wobble Age, the finale's doll hidden inside the lamp, Step Class footprints hidden behind the doll, Trudy's lean tutorial being impossible, and Enter on the session card missing the hub's time limit under load, which would have kept the game out of sessions.\n3. The scripted whole-game playthrough with bots on a stubbed pad. It got stuck on real flow bugs no single event test could show, including the first Wobble Age and the lean tutorial, and it runs the whole game in about a minute of machine time. Running the same bots through real keys and mouse came second: it found Log Reps unfair on keys, late in the build.\n4. The per-event rendered smoke run and the WebKit boot. Both passed the first time and changed nothing, though they remain worth keeping as guards.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. toonOutlinePass plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, the same on WebGPU and WebGL2. Rapier is also very fast: a minute of play at 120 Hz simulates in about a second.\n2. Yes. Rapier panics caught me most: disabling a core after removing its joint panicked world.step on a later dive, and every call after that reported a misleading recursive-use error. I first took that for a bug in my own test. I also guessed wrong on instance colours: a mesh first drawn before any setColorAt stays white for good.\n3. The balance core and the lane system, then a fixed-step game that runs entirely on sim time, so a test can play the whole game headlessly by calling advance().\n4. Knowing where a failure really started. A Rapier panic surfaces in a later call with a message about something else, and with software rendering each visual check takes a long time, so most judgement had to come from state instead of from looking.\n5. None.\n6. Make a Rapier panic a catchable error that names the call and the handle, instead of poisoning every later call on the page.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts about the machine and the hub, and a plan whose numbers were measured rather than guessed.\n2. How to write a source with a space in the file name, and what the engine's native style and asset library mean for Three.js, which has neither. The plan answered the second.\n3. Judging feel with no player. Scripts can prove an event is winnable, losable and separates skill levels, but not that it is fun. Bots also agreed too easily until I gave them human faults.\n4. Say how to tell memory pressure from other runs apart from a real crash in practice, since a shared machine with full swap can kill a long test. Ask for one short recorded video of play, so that feel has at least one more check.\n\nEverything is in `work/`, and nothing is committed to git:\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442/` (dev files; the issue log is `notes/issues.md`)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":876,"cache_read_input_tokens":925675,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":876},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6048a4b4-78b7-4200-a8b0-b745f5bb937e","uuid":"aa460807-adc7-4e96-be46-4d3c832f65cc","timestamp":"2026-11-05T15:05:40.991Z","request_id":"req_011CgjzE12kBnLNnLLXVVwVP"}
