{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgk5VyqaJLj1BWjFqXkFx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Fib is finished and everything TASK.md asks for is handed in, but my last \"12 of 12\" check overstated things.** Going back over it turned up real bugs, which are now fixed. The full suite on the final build gives **2093 passed, 0 failed**, with the same checksum before and after the run (`dd62d791b28bcf71`). The About screen matches `exit-interview.json`: all 29 values, nothing missing.\n\n## What the \"12 of 12\" got wrong\n- **A test that couldn't fail:** \"a slider changes the running event\" compared a number with itself, both in my one-off script and in the real suite.\n- **Lines not checked live:** that script marked four facts as passed without testing them: originality, the hub harness, the full suite and the chat reply. It also ran on the previous build and wasn't repeated on this one, so the evidence for this build is the full suite.\n\n## What I fixed\n| Problem | Fix | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| The slider test proved nothing | New `tools/test-sliders.mjs` measures every slider's effect on the running game, including d-pad, mouse and reset | All pass |\n| Island rest tilt did nothing until something else re-synced the island | The slider and reset both sync it | Takes effect at once |\n| Pumping the Springboard longer earned nothing, because of a clamp | Only the visible board stops at 0.7 m now | 4.8 / 6.5 / 7.5 m/s (plan 4.7 / 6.5 / 7.6) |\n| All Springboard bots pumped equally well, and sometimes tried an impossible dive | Bots now pump late according to skill, and only try dives they can finish | Launch 5.7 / 3.6 / 3.0 m/s (plan 5.4 / 3.8 / 3) |\n| High dives left the top of the picture once the clamp was gone | The camera rises with the diver and comes back for the splash | Checked by script and by eye |\n\nMy worry that Springboard scored the same on every seed was a false alarm from an old log.\n\n## What's still open\n- **Needs a real person or real hardware:** a controller, a TV, human ears and a human player. So whether it's fun, and the art, tone and originality, are still my judgement, as the plan allows.\n- **Plan numbers not all re-measured:** that clamp got past my earlier audit. I searched the events and physics for similar clamps and found none, but I didn't re-measure every figure in the plan.\n- **Weak bot results:**\n    - On Still Water, the clumsy bot scores 12 on keys and 15 on mouse, against 78 on the stick. Watch this first when someone plays.\n    - On Poses, the clumsy bot on keys scores 39, just under the Adequate medal.\n    - On Tippy Raft, the bots barely separate (50 / 40 / 40). The plan does say skill \"helps and doesn't decide it\".\n- **Commit:** nothing is committed, since you didn't ask for that.\n- **Other processes:** three running processes turned out to belong to another agent's run, so I left them alone; none of mine are still running.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 04:44:42\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression\n- **Perspective:** Third person: framed stage cameras per event, and an orbitable chase camera on the island\n- **Tech:** Three.js R180 (three 0.180.0) on the WebGPU backend with WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon materials and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3 (rapier3d-compat), Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Fredoka 5.3.0 (fontsource), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** Cel-shaded ink filter over a sun-bleached wooden-toy resort; every model made in code from Three.js primitives and toon materials\n- **Animation:** Three.js AnimationMixer clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean, bobble and breath from the physics, Rapier ragdolls built from the rig\n- **Audio:** Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices with every word on screen; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen with Group Class\n- **Testing:** Allowed: as much headless testing as wanted, scripts that play the game and check its state including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command printing only failures and a one-line count, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: node tools/test.mjs with eight groups and about 2,090 checks: whole-game playthrough by bots on a stubbed pad, every event and tier, balance tables over three seeds, the same bots again through real key and pointer events, every verb on pad, keys and mouse, offline audio renders, the hub harness at 1, 2 and 4 players, bad network and host leaving, the 1080p layout audit, touch profiles, a WebGL2 boot in WebKit, plus a screenshot art pass. Not run: real controller, real TV, human ears, a human player.\n- **Research:** The AI Hub protocol README and harness files from the hub repository. No further research on the source beyond what PLAN.md already held.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 55 logged in notes/issues.md as they happened: 43 caught by test scripts, measurements and screenshots, 12 found by reading code paths and by checking the build line by line against the plan.\n2. **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 11: Hop presses lost during hit-stop, every Tug of Peace doll falling, the Springboard crash, two Rapier panics on later dives, divers teleporting in mid-air, the game stuck at the first Wobble Age, the finale's doll hidden inside the lamp, Step Class footprints hidden behind the doll, Trudy's lean tutorial being impossible, and Enter on the session card missing the hub's time limit under load, which would have kept the game out of sessions.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted whole-game playthrough with bots on a stubbed pad. It got stuck on real flow bugs no single event test could show, including the first Wobble Age and the lean tutorial, and it runs the whole game in about a minute of machine time. Running the same bots through real keys and mouse came second: it found Log Reps unfair on keys, late in the build.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The Dev Mode slider test as I first wrote it. It compared a number with itself, so it could never fail, and it stood in front of a slider that really did nothing until I replaced it with one that measures every slider in the running game. Among tests that could fail, the per-event rendered smoke run and the WebKit boot passed the first time and changed nothing, though they remain worth keeping as guards.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** toonOutlinePass plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, the same on WebGPU and WebGL2. Rapier is also very fast: a minute of play at 120 Hz simulates in about a second.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Rapier panics caught me most: disabling a core after removing its joint panicked world.step on a later dive, and every call after that reported a misleading recursive-use error. I first took that for a bug in my own test. I also guessed wrong on instance colours: a mesh first drawn before any setColorAt stays white for good.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The balance core and the lane system, then a fixed-step game that runs entirely on sim time, so a test can play the whole game headlessly by calling advance().\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Knowing where a failure really started. A Rapier panic surfaces in a later call with a message about something else, and with software rendering each visual check takes a long time, so most judgement had to come from state instead of from looking.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Make a Rapier panic a catchable error that names the call and the handle, instead of poisoning every later call on the page.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts about the machine and the hub, and a plan whose numbers were measured rather than guessed.\n2. **What was confusing?** How to write a source with a space in the file name, and what the engine's native style and asset library mean for Three.js, which has neither. The plan answered the second.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Judging feel with no player. Scripts can prove an event is winnable, losable and separates skill levels, but not that it is fun. Bots also agreed too easily until I gave them human faults.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how to tell memory pressure from other runs apart from a real crash in practice, since a shared machine with full swap can kill a long test. Ask for one short recorded video of play, so that feel has at least one more check.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`; to test again, run `node tools/test.mjs full` from the dev folder:\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442/` (dev folder)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2087,"cache_read_input_tokens":198496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2087},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6048a4b4-78b7-4200-a8b0-b745f5bb937e","uuid":"664a13bf-7e25-46b7-8367-1f1ad002c614","timestamp":"2026-11-05T16:15:03.771Z","request_id":"req_011Cgk5VyZDFoHTkEL2m5nFs"}
