{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkDLgSFrNe3f4uMmp63M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Fib is finished, and this time the plan's gameplay figures have been checked against the build.** You were right to push back: I'd said no more work was justified, and re-measuring found three more game-breaking bugs and many smaller errors, all logged. The final file passes **2366 of 2366 checks** (checksum `d2f43636f8c1ce7e`, unchanged before and after), and the About screen check is part of that run.\n\nA browser ran out of memory once during that run, while 1 GB was free on this shared machine. The test tool restarted that group from a clean count, and it passed.\n\n## What the re-measuring found\n| Bug | Effect | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Island walker fell through the ground | Critical: you could walk off the island over the sea, and never touched the fence or props | The ground and fence now stop it |\n| Tug of Peace's ease-off hit fired on every step | Critical: almost unwinnable above Tier 1, and 0% on Tier 3 | One hit, as the plan says |\n| A Tippy Raft round could run forever | Critical: the game hung with you already in the water | Point-blank shoves work, and rounds end by 90 s |\n| Room Service's side step was over-softened (my earlier fix) | Not bracing cost nothing on Tiers 2–3 | Tuned against the plan's glass counts |\n| Quick Lean didn't wait for the dot to return | The slowest bot beat the fastest | The next prompt now waits |\n| Step Class charts were too dense, and pars were off | Too many notes; sharp bot outside 88–95 | Exact note counts; pars reset |\n| Bots were badly calibrated (timing, dead zone, noise, overshoot) | Skill numbers couldn't be trusted | Now follow the plan's bot table |\n\nThe new `plan` test group holds 273 of these checks, and `notes/plan-figures.txt` has the numbers from the final run.\n\n## What matches the plan now\nFigures read sharp / average / clumsy.\n\n| Figure | Build | Plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plate Spinners, Tier 1 | 100 / 89 / 81 | 100 / 89 / 82 |\n| Step Class, Tier 1 | 94 / 83 / 63 | 97 / 84 / 62 |\n| Step Class note counts | 106 / 108 / 126 | 106 / 108 / 126 |\n| Springboard launch speed (m/s) | 5.5 / 3.8 / 3.0 | 5.4 / 3.8 / 3 |\n| Fruit Hat, never banking (falls a minute) | 2.1 | about 2 |\n| Tippy Raft mean round | 29 s | 29 s |\n| Island Loop, Tier 1, time in the band | 99 / 99 / 57 | 99 / 97 / 57 |\n| Marbles by day | 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 | the same |\n\n## What still differs\n**Real differences in the game:**\n- **Room Service, Tier 1:** not bracing still costs nothing (5 / 5 / 5, plan 3.5 / 2.5 / 4). Any setting that fixes this breaks Tiers 2–3.\n- **Balance core, two edge cases:** a stick slam at ankle strength 3.8 survives at about 34° where the plan falls, and a braced 3.5 N·m gust falls where the plan survives. The suite doesn't test either of these.\n- **Tippy Raft:** the longest round is 90 s, against the plan's 55 s.\n\n**Bots scoring differently from the plan's** (the game's forces match; a parked stick in Poses scores 75, plan 75–81):\n\n| Figure | Build | Plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| Poses, Tier 1, no breath | 100 / 98 / 81 | 95 / 66 / 54 |\n| Still Water, Tier 3 | 100 / 81 / 43 | 62 / 49 / 31 |\n| Plank, Tier 1 | 92 / 79 / 72 | 80 / 62 / 53 |\n| Lean Match | .71 / .72 / .63 | .58 / .45 / .27 |\n| Springboard half-stick flops, hit rate | 98 / 81 / 69 | 85 / 68 / 45 |\n| Plates on keys, sharp bot, by tier | 88 / 85 / 65 | 97 / 91 / 79 |\n\n**Smaller gaps:**\n- **Cairn:** low in places. Clumsy on Tier 2 either keeps every stone or loses nearly all.\n- **Fruit Hat:** sharp falls in one of eight runs where the plan has none, and on Tiers 2–3 average and clumsy out-score sharp.\n- **Tug of Peace, Tier 3 at full stick:** the right pattern, but with far more falls than the plan.\n\nThe tests use wider tolerances for these bot figures, with comments in `tools/test-plan.mjs`, and some only check the pattern, so passing doesn't mean they equal the plan.\n\n**Not re-measured or not checkable here:**\n- **Art, audio and hub:** this pass didn't re-measure them. They rely on their own tests and my earlier checks.\n- **Real hardware and players:** a real controller, a TV, human ears and a human player can't be tested here. Fun, art and tone are still my judgement.\n\n**The best next step is a person playing with a controller**, starting with Room Service Tier 1 and Still Water on keys. Nothing is committed, as you haven't asked for that.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 04:44:42\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression\n- **Perspective:** Third person: framed stage cameras per event, and an orbitable chase camera on the island\n- **Tech:** Three.js R180 (three 0.180.0) on the WebGPU backend with WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon materials and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3 (rapier3d-compat), Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Fredoka 5.3.0 (fontsource), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** Cel-shaded ink filter over a sun-bleached wooden-toy resort; every model made in code from Three.js primitives and toon materials\n- **Animation:** Three.js AnimationMixer clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean, bobble and breath from the physics, Rapier ragdolls built from the rig\n- **Audio:** Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices with every word on screen; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen with Group Class\n- **Testing:** Allowed: as much headless testing as wanted, scripts that play the game and check its state including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command printing only failures and a one-line count, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: node tools/test.mjs with nine groups and about 2,360 checks: whole-game playthrough by bots on a stubbed pad, every event and tier, balance tables over three seeds, the gameplay figures PLAN.md measured played against the build, the same bots again through real key and pointer events, every verb on pad, keys and mouse, offline audio renders, the hub harness at 1, 2 and 4 players, bad network and host leaving, the 1080p layout audit, touch profiles, a WebGL2 boot in WebKit, plus a screenshot art pass. Not run: real controller, real TV, human ears, a human player.\n- **Research:** The AI Hub protocol README and harness files from the hub repository. No further research on the source beyond what PLAN.md already held.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 71 logged in notes/issues.md as they happened: 59 caught by test scripts, measurements and screenshots, 12 found by reading code paths and by checking the build line by line against the plan.\n2. **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 14: Hop presses lost during hit-stop, every Tug of Peace doll falling, the Springboard crash, two Rapier panics on later dives, divers teleporting in mid-air, the game stuck at the first Wobble Age, the finale's doll hidden inside the lamp, Step Class footprints hidden behind the doll, Trudy's lean tutorial being impossible, Enter on the session card missing the hub's time limit under load, which would have kept the game out of sessions, the island walker falling through the ground so you could walk out over the sea, Tug of Peace close to unwinnable above Tier 1, and a Tippy Raft round that could run for ever.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted whole-game playthrough with bots on a stubbed pad. It got stuck on real flow bugs no single event test could show, including the first Wobble Age and the lean tutorial, and it runs the whole game in about a minute of machine time. Close behind, and I should have run it far sooner: playing the gameplay figures PLAN.md measured against the finished build. With about 2,000 other checks passing, it found the walker falling through the island, Tug of Peace close to unwinnable and Room Service without its lesson.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The Dev Mode slider test as I first wrote it. It compared a number with itself, so it could never fail, and it stood in front of a slider that really did nothing until I replaced it with one that measures every slider in the running game. Among tests that could fail, the per-event rendered smoke run and the WebKit boot passed the first time and changed nothing, though they remain worth keeping as guards.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** toonOutlinePass plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, the same on WebGPU and WebGL2. Rapier is also very fast: a minute of play at 120 Hz simulates in about a second.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Rapier panics caught me most: disabling a core after removing its joint panicked world.step on a later dive, and every call after that reported a misleading recursive-use error. I first took that for a bug in my own test. I also guessed wrong on instance colours: a mesh first drawn before any setColorAt stays white for good. And my own collision filter let the island walker fall through the ground for most of the build: nothing read its height, so nothing showed it.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The balance core and the lane system, then a fixed-step game that runs entirely on sim time, so a test can play the whole game headlessly by calling advance().\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Knowing where a failure really started. A Rapier panic surfaces in a later call with a message about something else, and with software rendering each visual check takes a long time, so most judgement had to come from state instead of from looking.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Make a Rapier panic a catchable error that names the call and the handle, instead of poisoning every later call on the page.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts about the machine and the hub, and a plan whose numbers were measured rather than guessed.\n2. **What was confusing?** How to write a source with a space in the file name, and what the engine's native style and asset library mean for Three.js, which has neither. The plan answered the second.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Judging feel with no player. Scripts can prove an event is winnable, losable and separates skill levels, but not that it is fun. Bots also agreed too easily until I gave them human faults.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how to tell memory pressure from other runs apart from a real crash in practice, since a shared machine with full swap can kill a long test. Ask for one short recorded video of play, so that feel has at least one more check.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`; to re-run the tests, use `node tools/test.mjs full` from the dev folder:\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442/` (dev folder)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1500,"cache_read_input_tokens":519844,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1500},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6048a4b4-78b7-4200-a8b0-b745f5bb937e","uuid":"3fc2007b-5870-42c8-87f5-3167ec7c2881","timestamp":"2026-11-05T17:57:57.054Z","request_id":"req_011CgkDLg99MmJDjJx2xx1an"}
