, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timel\n\n**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\", and these are as far as it goes:**\n- **Real hardware.** Nobody has played this with a real controller on a real TV. I can't confirm that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio (section 3 gives the fallback).\n- **Hearing.** Nobody has listened. Pitches, levels and readability are checked as signals; whether the music is good is not. Check it with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\n- **People.** The bots are a model of a person, and their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so skill results are estimates. Mouse play shares the stick's path, and was tested for input, not simulated for skill.\n- **The whole game.** The prototype integrates one event of thirteen, with a stand-in look. The rest is simulated, not built. All animation beyond a couple of test clips, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene, is yours. Whether it is fun is yours to judge.\n\n---\n\n...\n5:**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. Skill results are written sharp / average / clumsy, from the calibrated bots\n54:- **Never show** the source's name on screen (outside the title's joke and the About screen's Source line), nor use its product or feature names (balance board, the words it uses for its test, age or bank), its characters, its avatar sty\n82:- **Keys in balance events ✔: they move the asking ring, and it stays where you leave it.** Holding a key moves the ring at 1.6 units/s; releasing leaves it there, and C recentres. The ring must be clearly visible on the Balance Disc, be\n140:**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n331:- *Numbers ✔:* radius 6, losing 1 m every 12 s down to 2. Tilt model K 6, C 3.5, 14° for one doll at the rim, clamped to 26°. Slope push `tilt × 10`. Walk 3 m/s. Swell about every 6 s: adds 2.5 rad/s to the tilt velocity. Shove: within \n800:*balance:* run bots over 3 seeds per event at Tier 1 and assert: sharp reaches Exemplary (≥85) on average, average reaches Pleasant or Adequate, clumsy reaches at least Adequate (≥40), and a bot that parks the stick exactly on the goal \n1030:**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per even\n1033:1. *How many issues did testing catch?* About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\n1074:    \"About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\",\nT2 stick 0.85: share 0.58 lean 10.4 hit false no flail ok 15 | flail at 16:ok 15 18:ok 15 20:ok 15 22:ok 15 24:ok 15 26:ok 15 28:ok 15 30:ok 15 34:ok 15 38:ok 15\nT2 stick 0.95: share 0.67 lean 12.1 hit false no flail ok 17 | flail at 16:ok 17 18:ok 17 20:ok 17 22:ok 17 24:ok 17 26:ok 17 28:ok 17 30:ok 17 34:ok 17 38:ok 17\nT2 stick 1: share 0.72 lean 12.9 hit false no flail ok 18 | flail at 16:ok 18 18:ok 18 20:ok 18 22:ok 18 24:ok 18 26:ok 18 28:ok 18 30:ok 18 34:ok 18 38:ok 18\nT3 stick 0.95: share 0.66 lean 11.9 hit false no flail ok 17 | flail at 16:ok 17 18:ok 17 20:ok 17 22:ok 17 24:ok 17 26:ok 17 28:ok 17 30:ok 17 34:ok 17 38:ok 17\nT3 stick 1: share 0.71 lean 12.7 hit false no flail ok 18 | flail at 16:ok 18 18:ok 18 20:ok 18 22:ok 18 24:ok 18 26:ok 18 28:ok 18 30:ok 18 34:ok 18 38:ok 18","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014ZdeJJ3YLcUecUhLvtgBCr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
