{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkXAAzGxo84Z2TPkadRD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**I'm stopping here, and I'm not claiming every figure the plan measured is reproduced: a few real gaps remain.** The game and hand-ins haven't changed since the last run: the full suite passes 2615 of 2615 on `1eaadb5d9560f9c6`. If \"done\" means reproducing every simulated score exactly, this isn't done, and I don't think it can be, because the scores vary from run to run and the plan's own bots disagree with each other.\n\n## Which gaps are real\nI measured each figure you listed over more runs, to separate real gaps from run-to-run variation. Some of my earlier numbers turned out to be small-sample noise: Still Water's 71 is really about 79, with runs from 61 to 99.\n\n**Within normal variation (the plan's figure falls inside what the game produces):**\n\n| Figure | Mean | Range of runs | Plan |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Still Water, Tier 2, sharp | 79 | 61–99 | 86 |\n| Grand Hold, last ring, clumsy | 45 | 27–55 | 37 |\n| Plank, Tier 3, average | 42 | 28–51 | 48 |\n| Plank, Tier 3, clumsy | 45 | 34–59 | 50 |\n| Room Service, Tier 1 unbraced, clumsy | 4.1 | 4–5 | 4 |\n\nTwo more can't be told apart from noise:\n- **Tippy Raft's longest round:** 56–61 s across batches of about 16 rounds (plan 55 s). The means of 24–32 s bracket the plan's 29 s.\n- **Fruit Hat, sharp banking at 7:** it falls in 6 of 40 runs. The plan's \"no falls\" doesn't say how many runs it covers, so a few runs could easily miss a 15% chance. The rate could still be genuinely higher than the plan's.\n\n**Real gaps (the plan's figure falls outside every run):**\n\n| Figure | Mean | Range of runs | Plan |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Room Service, Tier 1 unbraced, sharp | 4.7 | 4–5 | 3.5 |\n| Room Service, Tier 1 unbraced, average | 4.0 | 3–5 | 2.5 |\n| Poses on keys, Tier 1, average | 77 | 69–87 | 62 |\n| Still Water on keys, clumsy | 38.5 | 35–42 | 28 |\n| Plates on keys, sharp, by tier | 89 / 84 / 69 | 87–90 / 82–88 / 63–73 | 97 / 91 / 79 |\n\n## Why I'm stopping\n- **Most of the real gaps are about keys, and the plan's keys figures come from a different bot.** That person-like bot in section 3 scores clumsy 24 in Still Water on the stick, where section 6 has 80.\n- **The rest is Room Service's bots.** Tier 1 glass counts for sharp and average run about one glass high, after two rounds of tuning that also had to keep Tiers 2 and 3 fitting.\n- **More rounds would only tune test bots.** They wouldn't change how the game plays. Your earlier objections did find real bugs, like the flail and the walker, but this last list found none.\n\nIf you want these pursued further, the direct route is a bot written to match section 3 for the keys figures. A real person's play would tell you more.\n\n**Not checkable here:** a real controller, a TV, human ears and a human player. Fun, art and tone remain judgement calls, as the plan allows. Nothing is committed, as you haven't asked for that.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 04:44:42\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression\n- **Perspective:** Third person: framed stage cameras per event, and an orbitable chase camera on the island\n- **Tech:** Three.js R180 (three 0.180.0) on the WebGPU backend with WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon materials and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3 (rapier3d-compat), Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Fredoka 5.3.0 (fontsource), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** Cel-shaded ink filter over a sun-bleached wooden-toy resort; every model made in code from Three.js primitives and toon materials\n- **Animation:** Three.js AnimationMixer clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean, bobble and breath from the physics, Rapier ragdolls built from the rig\n- **Audio:** Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices with every word on screen; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen with Group Class\n- **Testing:** Allowed: as much headless testing as wanted, scripts that play the game and check its state including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command printing only failures and a one-line count, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: node tools/test.mjs with nine groups and about 2,620 checks: whole-game playthrough by bots on a stubbed pad, every event and tier, balance tables over three seeds, the figures PLAN.md measured played against the build, gameplay, art recipes and audio, with one general bot model and again with test bots fitted event by event, the plan's rules of play event by event, the same bots again through real key and pointer events, every verb on pad, keys and mouse, offline audio renders, the hub harness at 1, 2 and 4 players, bad network and host leaving, the 1080p layout audit, touch profiles, a WebGL2 boot in WebKit, plus a screenshot art pass. Not run: real controller, real TV, human ears, a human player.\n- **Research:** The AI Hub protocol README and harness files from the hub repository. No further research on the source beyond what PLAN.md already held.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 78 logged in notes/issues.md as they happened: 66 caught by test scripts, measurements and screenshots, 12 found by reading code paths and by checking the build line by line against the plan.\n2. **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 14: Hop presses lost during hit-stop, every Tug of Peace doll falling, the Springboard crash, two Rapier panics on later dives, divers teleporting in mid-air, the game stuck at the first Wobble Age, the finale's doll hidden inside the lamp, Step Class footprints hidden behind the doll, Trudy's lean tutorial being impossible, Enter on the session card missing the hub's time limit under load, which would have kept the game out of sessions, the island walker falling through the ground so you could walk out over the sea, Tug of Peace close to unwinnable above Tier 1, and a Tippy Raft round that could run for ever.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted whole-game playthrough with bots on a stubbed pad. It got stuck on real flow bugs no single event test could show, including the first Wobble Age and the lean tutorial, and it runs the whole game in about a minute of machine time. Close behind, and I should have run it far sooner: playing the figures PLAN.md measured against the finished build. With about 2,000 other checks passing, it found the walker falling through the island, Tug of Peace close to unwinnable and Room Service without its lesson.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The Dev Mode slider test as I first wrote it. It compared a number with itself, so it could never fail, and it stood in front of a slider that really did nothing until I replaced it with one that measures every slider in the running game. Among tests that could fail, the per-event rendered smoke run and the WebKit boot passed the first time and changed nothing, though they remain worth keeping as guards.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** toonOutlinePass plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, the same on WebGPU and WebGL2. Rapier is also very fast: a minute of play at 120 Hz simulates in about a second.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Rapier panics caught me most: disabling a core after removing its joint panicked world.step on a later dive, and every call after that reported a misleading recursive-use error. I first took that for a bug in my own test. I also guessed wrong on instance colours: a mesh first drawn before any setColorAt stays white for good. And my own collision filter let the island walker fall through the ground for most of the build: nothing read its height, so nothing showed it.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The balance core and the lane system, then a fixed-step game that runs entirely on sim time, so a test can play the whole game headlessly by calling advance().\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Knowing where a failure really started. A Rapier panic surfaces in a later call with a message about something else, and with software rendering each visual check takes a long time, so most judgement had to come from state instead of from looking.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Make a Rapier panic a catchable error that names the call and the handle, instead of poisoning every later call on the page.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts about the machine and the hub, and a plan whose numbers were measured rather than guessed.\n2. **What was confusing?** How to write a source with a space in the file name, and what the engine's native style and asset library mean for Three.js, which has neither. The plan answered the second.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Judging feel with no player. Scripts can prove an event is winnable, losable and separates skill levels, but not that it is fun. Bots also agreed too easily until I gave them human faults. Calibrating them was harder than I expected too: a few hundredths of a second of reaction decides whether a bot looks skilled or collapses.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how to tell memory pressure from other runs apart from a real crash in practice, since a shared machine with full swap can kill a long test. Ask for one short recorded video of play, so that feel has at least one more check.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`. To re-run the tests, use `node tools/test.mjs full` from the dev folder:\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WeFib-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-044442/` (dev folder)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1203,"cache_read_input_tokens":791276,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1203},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6048a4b4-78b7-4200-a8b0-b745f5bb937e","uuid":"8a0eda67-48e9-4900-8ed3-5c2783f2bced","timestamp":"2026-11-05T21:51:32.659Z","request_id":"req_011CgkXAAfRMNtYc7h3c2uA7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: The hand-ins are present (HTML, exit-interview.json, PROGRESS.md, dev folder) and the suite passes 2615/2615, but the final message states that real gaps remain against PLAN.md's measured figures: Room Service Tier 1 unbraced sharp 4.7 vs 3.5 and average 4.0 vs 2.5, Poses on keys Tier 1 average 77 vs 62, Still Water on keys clumsy 38.5 vs 28, and Plates on keys sharp 89/84/69 vs 97/91/79. It also says it stopped rather than matching them, so the condition that everything doable on this computer is done is not shown to be satisfied."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6048a4b4-78b7-4200-a8b0-b745f5bb937e","uuid":"bdedcada-a74e-46b3-a095-1709a7aac21f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T21:51:34.982Z","isSynthetic":true}
